  "strategy": "Approach and rationale: Instead of relying solely on polynomial multiple-lobe initialization, the improved strategy leverages exponential response building and adaptive gradient regularization. This targets sequences with enhanced time-frequency localization properties, which could lower the max autocorrelation. Dual-space projection onto natural logarithm exists (not enforced), so changes must respect linearity while improving extremal bounds.\n\nMathematical specification: For sequences a (length n), we require:\n1. Non-negativity: a[i] >= 0 for all i\n2. Upper bound scaling: a[i] <= 1000.0 for all i\n3. Objective minimization: O(a) = 2 * n * max(autoconv(a)[k] for k in range(2*n-1)) / (sum(a))^2\n\nThe formulation shifts from pure harmonic expansions to probability density frameworks where the exponential of autoconvolution values models system responses.\n\nImplementation procedure:\n- Structured exponentials: Create base sequences using e^(-x^2) or sinc-like functions\n- Adaptive regularization: Localize high magnitude coefficients\n- External constraints: Use analytical bounds formed via light-cone propagation optimization within valid matrix spaces\n- Transcoding: Convert sampled sequences to binary trees for accelerated evaluation\n- Random baseline checks: Fallback to power-of-two tree trunks if optimization stalls\n\nBuild and validate matrices by projecting gradient norms onto the dual equation space:\n\ndef construct_exponential_base():\n    # [Implementation details]\n\nSearch choices and useful variations:\n- Exponential envelopes for coefficients: Exponent based on distances to optimal points\n- Dual-space gradient descent using analytical logarithmic derivatives\n- Chebyshev resampling for near-optimal tuning\n- Pareto-smoothed optimization trees for boundary handling\n\nPrioritize sequences built from exponential kernels with adaptive damping factors.\n\nCompute budget and robustness:\n- Estimate dense matrix operations: Convolve requires FFT equivalent\n- Monitor dual-space convergence: Reserve 70% time for structured kernels\n- Fallback mechanism using binary fractal searches (trees) in 5 minutes if needed\n\nValidation and return contract:\n- Verify all integers in the sequence against double-differential extrema\n- Re-evaluate the object function using full convolution mode and step-by-step numerical checks\n- Return lowest recorded O(a) within finite runtime frames (90th percentile safe limits)\n- Ensure positivity via thresholding at 1e-12 epsilon\n\nThe key innovation is switching from amplitude-driven to transform-equivalent localization, with fallbacks ensuring validity under pressure.",
